# Line endings are part of a file's identity here, because the case-study packages
# publish sha256 hashes of their own inputs and check them back. Without this, a
# Windows checkout converts committed LF text into CRLF in the working tree, the
# manifest records the hash of the converted bytes, and the same commit then fails
# its own reproducibility check on Linux -- which is exactly why the integrity
# workflow had never passed.
#
# Declaring LF makes the working tree match the blob on every platform, so a hash
# taken of a file on disk is a hash of what the repository actually holds.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats must never be touched by end-of-line conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.rar binary
*.gz binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.gdb binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.parquet binary
